"use strict";(self.webpackChunkultimatelearning=self.webpackChunkultimatelearning||[]).push([[758],{5758:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"topic":{"id":"collections","title":"Java Collections Framework","overviewSimple":"Think of a huge toy box with compartments for different toys \u2014 cars, dolls, and blocks. Collections in Java organize data like this, making it easy to store, retrieve, and manage multiple items.","overviewTechnical":["The Java Collections Framework provides a set of interfaces, implementations, and algorithms to work with groups of objects.","Core interfaces include List, Set, Map, Queue, and Deque.","Implementations include ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet, HashMap, LinkedHashMap, PriorityQueue, etc.","Collections allow dynamic resizing, iteration, sorting, searching, and thread-safe operations (e.g., ConcurrentHashMap).","Java also provides utility classes like Collections and Arrays for algorithms and manipulation."],"story":"Imagine you have a huge organizer for all your toys. Each type of toy has its special place. You can quickly find, add, or remove toys whenever you want. Collections work similarly for data in Java.","realWorldAnalogy":"Collections are like storage bins in your room \u2014 boxes for cars, baskets for dolls, trays for blocks, helping you quickly organize and find your items.","keyPoints":["Organize objects efficiently","Dynamic resizing","Supports iteration and searching","Multiple implementations for different use-cases","Thread-safe variants available"],"exampleSimple":"List<String> fruits = new ArrayList<>();\\nfruits.add(\\"Apple\\");\\nfruits.add(\\"Banana\\");","exampleTechnical":["import java.util.*;\\n\\npublic class CollectionDemo {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        List<String> fruits = new ArrayList<>();\\n        fruits.add(\\"Apple\\");\\n        fruits.add(\\"Banana\\");\\n        for(String fruit : fruits){\\n            System.out.println(fruit);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}","import java.util.*;\\n\\npublic class MapDemo {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();\\n        map.put(1, \\"One\\");\\n        map.put(2, \\"Two\\");\\n        map.forEach((k,v) -> System.out.println(k + \\": \\" + v));\\n    }\\n}"],"extraNotes":["Always choose the right implementation based on performance requirements (e.g., ArrayList vs LinkedList).","Use generics to enforce type safety."],"bestPracticesSimple":["Use List for ordered collections","Use Set to avoid duplicates","Use Map for key-value pairs"],"bestPracticesAdvanced":["Prefer ArrayList for random access, LinkedList for frequent insertions/deletions","Use ConcurrentHashMap for multi-threaded access","Leverage utility methods from Collections and Arrays classes"]}}')}}]);